## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to design scalable, resilient multi-region systems with per-tenant isolation, hot-tenant mitigation, and cost control—core competencies for distributed systems engineers at senior levels.

## Key Concepts

- Time-based shard design and tenant isolation strategies
- Multi-region replication with quorum-based consistency
- Hot-tenant mitigation and dynamic shard rebalancing
- Data lifecycle management: tiered storage, retention, compaction, and archival

## Code Example

```javascript
function shardKey(tenantId, timestamp) {
  const hourBucket = Math.floor(timestamp / 3600000);
  return `${tenantId}:${hourBucket}`;
}

class TimeSeriesStore {
  constructor(regions, replicationFactor = 3) {
    this.regions = regions;
    this.replicationFactor = replicationFactor;
    this.quorum = Math.floor(replicationFactor / 2) + 1;
  }

  async write(tenantId, timestamp, data) {
    const key = shardKey(tenantId, timestamp);
    const replicas = this.selectReplicas(key);
    const writes = replicas.map(region => this.writeToRegion(region, key, data));
    await Promise.all(writes);
    return this.verifyQuorum(writes);
  }

  selectReplicas(shardKey) {
    return this.regions.slice(0, this.replicationFactor);
  }

  async read(tenantId, timestamp) {
    const key = shardKey(tenantId, timestamp);
    const replicas = this.selectReplicas(key);
    const reads = replicas.map(region => this.readFromRegion(region, key));
    return this.mergeResults(await Promise.all(reads));
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to design a multi-IdP, tenant-scoped token flow with replay protection and cross-tenant semantics.

## Key Concepts
- Normalization of tokens across IdPs
- Tenant-scoped metadata cache
- JWT structure and claims
- Replay protection with Redis TTL
- Clock skew handling and cross-tenant semantics

## Code Example
```javascript
// Issuance (pseudocode)
function issueToken(principal, tenant) {
  const jti = crypto.randomUUID();
  const payload = { iss: tenant, sub: principal.sub, aud: 'api', scope: principal.scope, iat: now(), exp: now()+600, jti, nonce: crypto.randomBytes(16).toString('hex'), tenant };
  const token = signJWT(payload, tenantKey);
  redis.set(`nonce:${tenant}:${jti}`, '1', 'EX', 600);
  return token;
}

// Verification (pseudocode)
function verifyToken(token) {
  const payload = verifyJWT(token);
  if (!redis.exists(`nonce:${payload.tenant}:${payload.jti}`)) throw new Error('Replay');
  return payload;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale nonce storage across millions of tenants?
- How would you rotate IdP keys and invalidate old tokens?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to prevent token replay across tenants and regions, a critical security risk in multi-IdP environments.

## Key Concepts

- Anti-replay: JTI and nonce checks per tenant
- Per-tenant isolation and TTL caches
- Cross-region cache synchronization (Redis cluster)
- Revocation semantics and monitoring

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo replay check
async function isReplay(tenant, id) {
  const key = `replay:${tenant}:${id}`;
  const exists = await redis.get(key);
  if (exists) return true;
  await redis.set(key, '1', 'EX', TTL);
  return false;
}
```

